# Copy to .env and fill in. Everything above the modes is required; then pick the
# one mode below that matches how TLS reaches your users, and uncomment that block.

# The address browsers use to reach Keycloak. Not a bind address: Keycloak already
# listens on all interfaces. This string becomes part of every user's identity, so
# changing it later invalidates existing logins. Prefer a name over an IP, because
# a name can be repointed and can later hold a certificate.
#   localhost              local development, browser on the same machine
#   192.168.1.50           reachable by IP on your network
#   auth.home.arpa         a name you resolve yourself (needs your own DNS)
#   auth.example.com       a domain you own
FENNIXS_HOSTNAME=localhost

# Generate each with: openssl rand -base64 32
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
AUTH_DB_PASSWORD=

# A temporary Keycloak admin, created on the first start only and ignored on every
# start after that, so editing these later changes nothing. Generate the password
# like the two above. Once the stack is running: sign in, create your own admin
# account, delete this temporary one from inside Keycloak, then remove these two
# lines. Until you do, a working admin credential sits here in plain text.
KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=


# --- Mode 1: plain HTTP, reached over a VPN or trusted LAN -------------------
# The tunnel provides the encryption. Keycloak already permits plaintext from
# 10.x, 172.16-31.x and 192.168.x, so a WireGuard or OpenVPN tunnel handing out one
# of those needs the first line only. Tailscale and anything else assigning from
# 100.64.0.0/10 is refused and needs the second line as well, otherwise logins fail
# with "HTTPS required". Leave the second line out if you do not need it.
# Do not expose the published port to the internet in this mode.
#
# FENNIXS_SSL_REQUIRED applies on the FIRST START ONLY. It is stored in the realm,
# so editing it later does nothing and nothing says so. Change it afterwards in
# Keycloak: Realm settings > General > Require SSL. If you add a certificate later,
# set it back to "external" there, or this instance keeps accepting plaintext.
#ENABLE_HTTP=true
#FENNIXS_SSL_REQUIRED=none


# --- Mode 2: Keycloak terminates TLS itself ---------------------------------
# FENNIXS_CERTS_DIR is a directory on this host and must already exist, otherwise
# Docker creates it owned by root and Keycloak then reports that it cannot read
# the certificate. The two paths below are inside the container: only the file
# names are yours, the directory is fixed. Using a Java keystore instead of a PEM
# pair? Drop the two paths and set KC_HTTPS_KEY_STORE_FILE to a file in the same
# directory.
#FENNIXS_CERTS_DIR=/etc/letsencrypt/live/auth.example.com
#KC_HTTPS_CERTIFICATE_FILE=/opt/keycloak/conf/certs/fullchain.pem
#KC_HTTPS_CERTIFICATE_KEY_FILE=/opt/keycloak/conf/certs/privkey.pem


# --- Mode 3: a reverse proxy in front terminates TLS ------------------------
# Caddy, nginx, Traefik, a Cloudflare Tunnel. Keycloak speaks HTTP on the internal
# network and the proxy holds the certificate. Set FENNIXS_HOSTNAME above to the
# public https:// URL. Leave FENNIXS_SSL_REQUIRED alone: the proxy connects from a
# private address, so the default already permits it.
#ENABLE_HTTP=true


# --- Not set here -----------------------------------------------------------
# Account self-registration is closed and is changed in Keycloak, under
# Realm settings > Login > User registration. SMTP, which password reset and
# email verification both need, is set under Realm settings > Email.


# --- Optional ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Only if 8080 or 8443 are already taken on this host. These change the host side
# of the mapping; the ports inside the container do not move.
#FENNIXS_HTTP_PORT=8080
#FENNIXS_HTTPS_PORT=8443
